6. Field policy\
6.1 The governing test: does the platform builder read this key?\
Signed off 2026-07-17. Tier assignment is not a matter of taste. One question decides it:\
A field is editable if changing it changes what the emulator does. A field is derived if it does not.\
The corollary matters more than the rule. CypressIde almost certainly hardcodes its own PCI config header -- 0x1080:0xc693 lives in the C++ class, because config space is part of the device model. If the manifest parser never reads pci_devices[*].vendor, then that key is not configuration at all. It is an echo: documentation of what the class already does, written into the file for human legibility.\
Making an echo field editable is strictly worse than making it readonly. The user types a value, nothing happens, and the manifest now lies. That is the identity drift of Section 1 with a text box attached.\
T-00 (Section 10) is the evidence gate for this section. No policy table lands until a pass over the manifest parser establishes which keys it actually consumes. The table in 6.4 is a prediction pending that pass, and every row is subject to it.\
Predicted partition:\
Class\
Keys\
Read -> editable\
hose, bus, slot, func, interrupt_pin, option_rom, address, byte, size (nvram), media, media_kind, storage.*, name\
Echo -> derived\
vendor, device, class_code, bars[*]\
Two consequences already accepted:\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'95\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0interrupt_pin moves to editable. If IRQ routing reads it, it is board wiring (a platform property), not silicon identity (a device property). This reverses the initial classification -- see Q-2, closed.\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'95\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0bars[*] stays derived. BAR size is decided by the silicon; the device decodes what it decodes. freeform: true remains the escape hatch for generic -- see Q-4, closed.\
On keeping the echo fields at all. If model: cypress_ide is sufficient to reconstruct vendor / device / class_code, those keys are pure redundancy and therefore a drift source. They are kept because they buy manifest legibility -- the topology is readable without cross-referencing the catalog -- and V-07 makes the redundancy safe rather than dangerous. This is a deliberate trade, not an oversight. Every echoed field is a field that can disagree with the truth, and the validator is the only thing standing between those two states.\
6.2 The collision problem, and why policy is path-keyed\
The originating classification lists type under both readonly and selectable, kind under both readonly and selectable, and both device-name (readonly) and Name (selectable). These are not contradictions in the request -- they are evidence that the same leaf name means different things at different depths:\
pci_devices[*].bars[*].kind\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0 io | mem\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0 -> catalog-derived\
pci_devices[*].storage[*].type\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0 ata_disk | atapi_cdrom -> user choice\
iic_devices[*].class\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0 status | fru_eeprom | nvram\
iic_devices[*].size\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0 nvram byte count\'a0\'a0\'a0 -> user-editable\
pci_devices[*].bars[*].size\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0 BAR aperture\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0 -> catalog-derived\
iic_devices[*].size and pci_devices[*].bars[*].size are the clearest case: same leaf name, opposite policy.Therefore policy is keyed by path, never by leaf name. Path grammar:\
$.platform\
$.iic_devices[*].address\
$.pci_devices[*].bars[*].kind\
$.pci_devices[*].storage[*].media\
* matches any array index. Longest-literal-prefix wins on conflict. Unmatched paths fall through to P-2 passthrough.\
