7. Device catalog\
device_catalog.json is the reason the derived tier exists and is the heart of the tool. It declares each modelled device once:\
\{\
\'a0 "catalog_version": 1,\
\'a0 "pci_models": [\
\'a0\'a0\'a0 \{ "model": "cypress_ide", "vendor": "0x1080", "device": "0xc693",\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0 "class_code": "0x010100", "interrupt_pin": 0, "bars": [],\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0 "supports": ["storage"],\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0 "comment": "Cypress 82C693 IDE func1. Fixed legacy ports; no relocatable BARs." \},\
\'a0\'a0\'a0 \{ "model": "generic", "vendor": null, "device": null, "class_code": null,\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0 "freeform": true,\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0 "comment": "Escape hatch. All identity fields become user-authored." \}\
\'a0 ],\
\'a0 "platforms": ["DS10", "DS20", "ES40"]\
\}\
Behaviour:\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'95\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0Selecting model in the property pane populates every derived field from the catalog entry, marks them greyed, and shows the catalog comment as field-level help.\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'95\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0Conflict policy (P-3). If the loaded manifest's value for a derived field disagrees with the catalog, the tool does not overwrite. It renders the field with a conflict decoration, shows both values, and offers an explicit Adopt catalog value action. Nothing changes without a click.\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'95\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0freeform: true models (generic, and today's de500_tulip entry uses it) demote all derived fields to their natural editable tier. This is how hand-authored BARs remain authorable -- see Section 13 Q-4.\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'95\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0Unknown model in manifest: warn, treat as freeform, preserve everything.\
The catalog is hand-maintained in v1 and is the single point of truth for silicon identity. Future: generate it from the EmulatR device registry at build time so the manifest editor and the emulator cannot disagree. Out of scope for v1.\
7.1 Per-field override -- the bring-up escape hatch\
Signed off 2026-07-17. There is a real and recurring bring-up case that a strict derived policy would block: deliberately lying to the firmware to observe whether SRM enumerates differently. Presenting a wrong vendor ID on purpose is a legitimate diagnostic, and the iic_rcm_temp @ 0x9e root-cause is precedent for exactly that class of probe.\
The answer is not a general unlock. It is a per-field override:\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'95\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0Context menu on any derived field: Override catalog value.\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'95\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0The field demotes to its natural editable tier for that node only. The catalog value is retained alongside the override, not discarded.\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'95\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0The field renders in a visually distinctoverridden state -- not the greyed (catalog) treatment, and not an ordinary editable field either. It must be impossible to mistake a probe for a real value at a glance.\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'95\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0V-07 fires and stays in the warnings dock for as long as the override lives.\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'95\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0Revert to catalog value restores the derived state.\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'95\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0The override is persisted in the manifest as an ordinary value. The manifest format gains nothing; the tool remembers that the value disagrees with the catalog, because the catalog is right there to compare against.\
Rationale: a throwaway probe should not require polluting the catalog, but it also must not look identical to an authored value. The default stays coherent, and the experiment is loud instead of silent.\
